# Copy to scripts/local.sh (git-ignored) and fill in this site's values; config.sh reads it
# first and refuses to start with any of the required settings below left empty. A prepared
# site's real values are in docs/SITE.md (also git-ignored), not in this repository.

# --- Finding the interfaces and addresses (GB10 / DGX Spark) ---------------------------------
# A GB10 has two ConnectX-7 ports for the direct host-to-host cables, plus an ordinary LAN
# port for everything else. The rank traffic (NCCL's exchanges and the rendezvous) must run
# on the CX7 ports — if NCCL picks the LAN, everything still works but slowly.
#
# 1. Find the CX7 netdevs on each host:
#      lspci | grep -i connectx        # the two ConnectX-7 controllers (driver mlx5_core)
#      ethtool -i <dev> | grep driver  # mlx5_core confirms a CX7 device
#      ip -br link                     # their netdevs: on GB10s typically PCI-addressed
#                                      # names like enp1s0f1np1 or enP2p1s0f1np1; the LAN
#                                      # port is a different device
# 2. Cable CX7 port to CX7 port (the first cable to the peer's first port, and so on).
# 3. Give each cable its own private /24 subnet — no DHCP, no router, just static addresses
#    on both hosts (netplan or nmcli; the recipe does not manage host networking). Example:
#      head:   192.168.10.1/24 on the first cable, 192.168.20.1/24 on the second
#      worker: 192.168.10.2/24 and                 192.168.20.2/24
#    One cable works too — then there is one subnet and one netdev below.
# 4. Verify before starting: `ip -br addr` shows the rail devices UP on both hosts, and
#    `ping 192.168.10.2` from the head answers.

# --- required: site identity -----------------------------------------------------------------
WORKER=user@192.168.10.2         # rank 1's ssh target (key-based ssh); use the worker's
                                 # rail IP directly — do not rely on DNS or hostnames

# The ConnectX-7 rails: NCCL's bootstrap listens on the head's rail-1 address; NCCL
# discovers the rest from there. Required on both:
HEAD_FABRIC_ADDR=192.168.10.1    # the head's rail-1 address
WORKER_FABRIC_ADDR=192.168.10.2  # the worker's rail-1 address
NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=enp1s0f1np1,enP2p1s0f1np1
#                                 # comma-separated CX7 netdevs NCCL may use — the names from
#                                 # step 1 on THIS site; two rails on a two-cable site, one
#                                 # netdev on a one-cable site

# --- optional: pins and serving ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The TensorFold line to track: latest v1.* release by default; pin with a tag (or TF_COMMIT).
# TF_REF=v1.1.1

# The checkpoint: this line is EXL3 only (config.sh defaults to Mia-AiLab's EXL3 checkpoint
# + the DFlash2 drafter). The NVFP4 route has its own repo
# (GLM-5.3-Flash-NVFP4-2x-GB10-TensorFold-v1-testing).

# Serving (defaults in config.sh; the config default window is 40960 — the EXL3 route is
# unmeasured on this line at every window). The production recipe's zig-engine branch ran
# this checkpoint at 131072/8 on GB10s — a sane target once the first runs are green:
# CONTEXT=131072
# PARALLEL=8
# SERVED_NAME=GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3
# API_KEY=change-me                 # recommended; clients send it as Authorization: Bearer
# DRAFTS=0                          # the serial reference, no drafts
# DENSE_BITS=8                      # pack the dense linears to 8 bits at load (v1.1.1; try 6/4 after)
# HEAD_BITS=8                       # the head likewise
# PDL=1                             # programmatic dependent launch on the mm kernels
# PROMPT_K=shape                    # v1.1.1's K-slice regrouping opt-out (A/B vs 1.1.0 bits)
# CPUSET=0-4,10-14                  # pin to the GB10's efficiency cores (see #1033 in
#                                   # docs/LAUNCH.md): stops the spin/heat build-up on long runs

# NCCL_DEBUG=INFO                   # first bring-up: NCCL's view of the ranks and the rails
